WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:00.960
- [Onlooker] Ooh.

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:02.670
- [Narrator] Seconds, that's all it takes

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:05.220
for a train to come off the tracks,

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:06.510
(train derailing)

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.970
like this one in Springfield, Ohio.

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:09.870
It's just one

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:14.130
of the around a dozen derailments
already reported in 2023,

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:17.190
according to the Association
of American Railroads.

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:20.400
Over 1,000 trains derail every year.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.510
- Every single event that we
investigate is preventable.

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:26.370
- [Narrator] And while not all are severe,

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:29.070
derailments can happen in many ways.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.380
Here are some of the
biggest reasons trains

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.630
go off the tracks and what can be done

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:35.343
to make the system safer.

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:39.120
Derailments are the most
common train accidents

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.140
in the US.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.800
In 2022, 66% of accidents were classified

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.040
by the Federal Railroad
Administration as derailments.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:49.920
That's across all 600
plus railroad companies

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.020
in the country, in both rail yards,

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:55.530
where most derailments happen,
and out on the main lines.

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:59.040
And track issues are one
of the biggest causes,

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:01.110
That can be a track that's too wide,

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:03.546
a broken rail or a warped track.

00:01:03.546 --> 00:01:04.379
(train whistling)

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:05.700
Essentially, it's when.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.680
- The track isn't ready for the train.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.600
- [Narrator] Michael Hiller
is the deputy director

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.900
of the Railroad Investigation
Office at the NTSB.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:16.890
He investigated this 2017
derailment in Graettinger, Iowa

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:20.100
where 20 cars of a Union
Pacific train derailed

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:21.150
at a bridge.

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:25.110
That year, 14% of derailments
caused by track issues

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:28.110
were because of some sort
of a broken track piece.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.810
- The rail broke under
the middle of the train.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:33.480
There's a portion of the
train that's on the other side

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.090
of the bridge and it's safe,
but then there's a pile

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:39.120
of cars and then behind,
there's still a bunch

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:40.950
of cars that haven't derailed.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:42.570
- [Narrator] Some cars released ethanol,

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:44.700
causing the train to catch fire.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:46.710
The NTSB found that the track

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:50.610
in the area of the derailment
was inadequately maintained.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:55.530
- Every time it hit that zone,
it would just pound, pound

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:58.710
and pound that rail until
it eventually broke.

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:01.290
- [Narrator] In 2013, a
catastrophic derailment

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:03.840
in Bridgeport, Connecticut was also caused

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.430
by a broken track piece.

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:07.650
A Metro-North passenger train derailed

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:11.370
and collided with another
train, injuring 65 people.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:13.800
In this incident, compromised joint bars

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.390
that connect two different types

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:17.700
of rail together were fractured.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.800
- Once that joint bar is broken,

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.260
there's no longer a structure able

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:25.410
to support the safe movement of a train.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:28.440
- [Narrator] In both cases,
the NTSB made recommendations

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:31.350
emphasizing track
maintenance and inspection

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:34.830
but it's ultimately up to the
railroads to implement them.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:38.280
In response, Metro-North
updated its inspection protocol

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:39.270
and says it continues

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:42.240
to maintain a robust
track inspection program.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:44.580
And Union Pacific says its inspection

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:48.180
and maintenance policies go
above and beyond requirements.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.930
- As a track wears, it
does require inspection

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:55.980
and it does require maintenance,
and it's that inspection

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:00.090
that is really critical
to identify what types

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:04.680
of interventions are necessary
to keep it operating safe.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.170
- [Narrator] Track isn't
the only thing that breaks.

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:08.880
Among the major freight railroads,

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:11.550
equipment failures were
the most common cause

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:14.340
of mainline derailments in 2022.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:15.173
That was the case

00:03:15.173 --> 00:03:18.630
in the February derailment
in East Palestine, Ohio.

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:21.150
The NTSB's preliminary report said

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:22.920
that an overheated wheel bearing

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:24.930
likely caused the Norfolk Southern train

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:26.610
to go off the tracks.

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:28.710
Sparks under a rail car could be seen

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:32.130
on surveillance cameras miles
before the train derailed.

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:33.780
38 cars derailed,

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:36.750
including 11 that were
carrying hazardous materials

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:39.540
and caught fire, damaging 12 more.

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:42.630
The incident rattled the
town, angering residents

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:45.420
who still worry about
damages to the community,

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:48.570
including fear of possible
water contamination.

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:50.940
- We all just want answers.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:54.480
I think that's what everybody
here wants is just answers.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:56.280
- [Narrator] The Environmental
Protection Agency

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:59.070
has said local drinking water is safe.

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:02.250
It also ordered Norfolk
Southern to clean up the site.

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:05.130
The railroad also announced
new safety initiatives,

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:06.930
like adding more temperature sensors

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:09.390
to its tracks to alert a train's crew

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:11.580
of any trouble before it's too late.

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:13.710
Inspection and new sensors alone

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:15.600
can't prevent all derailments.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:18.180
Many are caused by human errors.

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:19.920
That can mean going too fast,

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.560
miscommunication, or misaligned switches.

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:25.470
The NTSB has tried to
address human errors,

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:27.270
including pushing for safeguards,

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:29.370
like positive train control.

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:31.830
PTC is a system that helps to slow

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:34.200
and stop trains to prevent accidents,

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:37.260
like collisions and overspeed derailments.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:42.260
- In my time, I've had at
least four investigations

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:45.360
where overspeeding in a curve resulted

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:48.870
in a derailment and a
very catastrophic outcome.

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:50.760
- [Narrator] In 2008, Congress mandated

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:53.730
that every major railroad
develop a PTC system

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:55.830
in the Rail Safety Improvement Act.

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:57.810
It took 12 years for all

00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:00.720
of the railroads to fully implement PTC.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.370
- Freight rail companies need
to stop fighting us every time

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:07.170
we try to do a regulation.

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:09.450
- [Narrator] Norfolk Southern
CEO said that the company

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:12.540
supports legislative efforts
to enhance rail safety.

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:14.910
In March, a bipartisan group of senators

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:16.680
proposed new legislation

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:18.840
that would tighten safety regulations

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:22.470
and increase fines, but
pushback is expected.

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:25.830
Industry Group AAR criticized
the proposed legislation,

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:29.310
saying some regulations,
like crew size requirements

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:31.053
wouldn't prevent future accidents.

00:05:31.053 --> 00:05:32.400
(train rumbling)

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.220
Overall, derailments are
happening less frequently

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:36.540
than they were a decade ago.

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:39.750
That's thanks in part to
advances in rail safety,

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:41.850
including positive train control.

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:43.380
But for some railroads, the rate

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:46.140
of derailments has
increased in recent years

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:49.293
as they've moved more
freight over fewer miles.

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:52.387
Still compared to other
modes of transportation.

00:05:52.387 --> 00:05:54.480
- [Michael Train travel is very safe.

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:58.200
If you compare highway
versus rail, the loss of life

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:01.920
is a lot greater on the
highway side versus rail.

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:03.150
- [Narrator] Hiller thinks there can be

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:05.460
more improvements when
it comes to rail safety.

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:06.840
- You have to have good maintenance.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:08.100
You have to have good training.

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:10.260
You have to have good organizations

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:13.470
that really embrace safety
as part of its culture.

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:16.700
It's achievable, but it's
gonna take a lot of work.

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:20.442
(pensive music)
(train whooshing)

